WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
Hello everyone and welcome to another game you requested.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.040
It's from the 1962 Curaçao Candidates Tournament.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.640
We've already shown Pal Benko vs Bobby Fischer.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.840
A very nice victory by Pal Benko with the Benko Opening or the Hungarian Opening, the 1. g3 move and

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.940
a lot of you said that you would like to also see his game against Mikhail Tal and

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.640
Well, let's just enjoy it. We haven't covered the 1962 Curaçao Candidates .

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.400
I'm planning to cover it at some point . Maybe as a part of the Tigran Petrosian Saga

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.540
as this is a candidates tournament that leads

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.820
to the World Chess Championship match, but maybe as a standalone tournament as it's such a nice tournament.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.059
But until I decide on that, let's just enjoy this game.

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:46.829
So Benko with the white pieces again,

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:54.269
we have 1. g3 the Benko Opening 1... g6 2. Bg2 Bg7 3. d4

00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:59.608
3... d6 4. e4 grabbing the center 4... Nf6

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.220
5. Ne2

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.400
5... O-O 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nbc3

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.940
7... c6 8. a4. So the position is similar to the position Fischer had

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.840
against Benko, also in the in the Curaçao Candidates.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.100
Although Fisher's knight was still on b8 and Fischer had a pawn on e5, but

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.740
what Fisher allowed was for Benko to push this pawn all the way to a5 whereas Tal doesn't allow it.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.140
Tal pushes a5 himself and he says that it's

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.300
too strong to allow white to push a5.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.620
So okay. 9. b3 similar like in the game against Fischer,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.800
he wants to develop this bishop to a3 to assume this strong diagonal.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.560
9... Re8 10. Ba3 Qc7 and it's interesting,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.980
this position has never happened again after it was played in this game.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:04.480
11. Qd2 preparing to connect rooks and only now 11... e5 now that he really controls the e-file.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.310
But, of course,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:12.060
Benko ignores it. 12. Rad1 exd4 13. Nxd4

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.130
13... Nc5. Tal finds an excellent outpost for this knight on c5

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:23.970
as white will be unable to push b4 due to the a5 pawn Tal pushed and also white doesn't have a d-pawn to

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.920
kick it away and it's very unlikely that you will give up your dark square bishop for this knight.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.440
So an excellent outpost for the knight. 14. f3 strengthening the e4 pawn

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:39.500
14... b6 Tal is now ready to develop the light square bishop as well. 15. Nde2

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.050
Now the queen and rook are ready to capture on d6.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:49.110
So Tal has to defend in some way. Now you could defend it with 15... Rd8

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.880
But Tal is greedy. Tal says I already developed this rook to e8

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.359
I want to play Bb7 maybe Ba6 and bring this rook over to

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.520
d8 without wasting any time so first he defends it with the 15... Bf8.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:08.480
But now with Bf8 this bishop no longer controls this excellent diagonal. So Benko transfers his own Bishop

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.480
to this excellent diagonal.  16. Bb2

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.720
16... Qe7. This is an odd move.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.900
I haven't really

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.239
seen the idea behind Qe7

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:27.600
Bb7 followed by Rad8 as he planned seems to be the way to go but

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:37.160
he prefers Qe7. And Benko takes this opportunity to get his knight into the game 17. Nd4

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.600
17. Kf4 was also a possibility, but he plays Kd4 as the queen no longer guards the c6 pawn

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:49.859
But this doesn't really bother Tal as he was planning to develop the bishop to b7 either way. 17... Bb7 and now

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.640
The knight also blocks the queen's attack towards the d6 pawn.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.880
So Tal doesn't have to worry about that.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:03.320
18. Rfe1 Bg7. Now the bishop can get back to this strong diagonal as the pawn is

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:11.399
not in danger of being captured. 19. f4 Rad8. Tal manages to

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.200
get his a rook to d8 as planned. 20. Bf3 now controlling

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.360
the g4 square not allowing the black knight to go there and also

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.080
making room for the king or the queen to occupy the g2 square. 20... Qd7

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:34.160
and now if you don't want to allow some Qh3 ideas, Benko prevents it with 21. Qg2 d5

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.340
Tal would be very happy if he can just trade everything on d5

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.730
Make his light square bishop come alive, but Benko of course is not interested in that.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.249
He says we're just gonna keep the center closed.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.179
22. e5 pushing the knight back 22... Nfe4

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.809
and now it seems like Tal is offering a pawn

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:58.260
but he's really not. Benko trades once 23. Nxd4 dxe4 and now even though it seems like he can capture here,

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:04.379
he really can't because if 24. Bxe4 Nxe4. It doesn't really matter what you capture the knight with.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.909
Let's say 25. Qxe4 c5 just wins the game as now you either lose the queen, of course

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.300
you're gonna move the queen, but then you lose the knight, so you're gonna be down a piece.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:16.230
So after 23... dxe4 Benko doesn't fall for it.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:22.920
He plays 24. Be2 Qe7, getting the queen away from this d file as it's just

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.529
not really doing all that much here. And now Benko brings the bishop back 25. Ba3

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.720
So really nice maneuvering with the bishop by Benko.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.680
First a3 then returning to b2 when the b2 diagonal became more important then now returning it

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:43.920
to a3 pinning the knight As Tal's queen is now on e7. And here Tal probably should have gone 25... Qc7.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.980
Even on the previous move Qc7 was better.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.440
Just so Ba3 doesn't do anything.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.820
But even here just retreating with Qc7 is best. But here

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:01.660
Tal played 25... f6. Now is this one of those positions where it's actually okay to play f6?

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.840
You will decide as you will pause the video here and try to find the best continuation for Benko in this position.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.300
Well, I give you a couple of seconds

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.220
So for those of you who were able to do it,

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:22.700
congratulations, you are an excellent punisher of an f6 move when it's not okay to play it in a position.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:29.339
And for those of you who just want to enjoy the show 26. Bc4+. And now, of course, you see that you cannot

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.850
interpose with the knight as the knight is pinned, you will lose the queen.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:37.349
You cannot go Kf8 because just Ne6+ pretty much ends the game.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:39.540
So Tal played 26... Kh8

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:45.059
27. Ne6 just the same as the knight still cannot be captured due to the pin.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.990
You have to move the rook the problem is

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:56.460
once this dark square bishop gets eliminated and it will be eliminated this bishop will once again return to this square

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.250
and then Benko will have these... Well, we can say it, a nice bishop pair from hell.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:08.219
27... Rd5 Tal decided it was better just to give up the rook that way. For example

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.940
if you play something like 27... Rxd1 28. Rxd1, then

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.719
there's not a good move once Nxg7.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.660
You're just gonna bring the Bb2 you're gonna capture on f6 open up this diagonal and it's just game over.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:27.840
So Tal decided to give up the the exchange with 27... Rd5 to at least get rid of one of the bishops.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.700
So Benko said, okay. It's a nice rook. I will have it.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:40.200
28. Bxd5 cxd5 and now of course you are getting rid of the dark square bishop. 29. Nxg7

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:47.560
29... Kxg7 30. exf6+ Qxf6

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:54.160
not allowing Bb2 as the queen now guards the b2 square, but it's not a problem 31. Qf2

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:57.839
You don't want to allow something like e3 and d4

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:05.639
for Tal to free his bishop. 31. Qf2 not allowing e3 31... Ne6. Now Tal,

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:12.029
he's down the exchange, but he does have a strong center and if he can push d4 and e3

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:16.049
and if this bishop comes alive, it could become very dangerous for Benko.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:22.560
So here Tal is saying basically, well bluffing actually, that Benko cannot capture on b6, that it's too dangerous.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.060
But Benko says I'm not tricking you. I know your style

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.239
I'm just gonna capture it because this is a nice free pawn.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.430
So he played 32. Qxb6 and now, of course, Tal has to do something about the bishop.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:39.960
32... Ba8 33. Bd6

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.160
Benko is now preparing Be5 to win the queen here.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:48.520
So Tal just gets the queen out of the way. 33... Qf5

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:56.980
Now already again if Tal can get these two moves in then the bishop, the queen there might be something here

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.820
But 34. Bxa5. Benko just grabs another pawn. He says there's nothing there.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:10.540
Tal moves the king even further to safety. 34... Kh6 35. c4

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.580
starting to push the pawns because if you capture then just a nice queen trade.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:21.369
But as Benko is up in material Tal cannot afford a queen trade. 35... Rd8

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:28.359
Attacking the bishop, 36. Be7 attacking the rook and also preparing Bg5 if needed.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:32.680
This will come with check. So Tal says okay here

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:40.600
it's all or nothing. 36... e3. Tal just pushes the pawn and he asks are you maybe interested in a nice rook on d8?

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:48.580
Well, could you capture it? Sure, you could capture it. There isn't all that much poison there but there's no need

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:53.580
to give Tal more than you need because a lot of players did this and, you know, it always goes wrong.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:01.509
37. Rxe3 just getting rid of that pawn and getting the rook to a more useful square.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:08.950
37... Re8. The rook overstayed its welcome on d8, 38. Bg5+.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.090
Of course any trades are not in your favour. Now the rook on e8 is unguarded

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:20.079
38... Kg7 39. Bde1 Benko claims the e-file for himself.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:27.000
And again Tal says it's all or nothing. 39... Nxg5. Tal tries giving up the rook on e8

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:33.820
For some Nf3 action and even though you could capture it allow 40... Nf3+, it's really hard to do

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:41.520
to actually blunder the game. Even if something like 41.Kg2 Nxe1+ 42. Rxe1

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:50.120
You deliver this check in hopes of picking up the queen on a5 it doesn't worry because just 43. Qxa8 so it's

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:56.200
very hard to blunder this game. But you're playing against Tal you don't want to

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:04.629
complicate it too much. So he just played 40. fxg5 Rf8

00:11:04.629 --> 00:11:10.920
So Tal now has decent pressure along the f-file and if he manages to free the light square bishop,

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:16.460
Tal maybe could do something but Benko here played 41. Qa7+

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:20.900
and in fact it was in this position that Mikhail Tal resigned the game on move 41

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:23.360
as there really is nothing more to do here.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:26.260
Because once the king moves you can't go here now,

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.869
the pawn is guarding that square you have to go to g8 or h8 doesn't really matter.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.710
41... Kh8. If you block with the rook, of course, you lose the bishop with check.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.180
So 41... Kh8

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:40.730
42. Re7 and there's no defense. Just Rxh7 is coming and

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.639
with the queen on a7 you also guard the f2 square. So you can't even do a

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.310
nice little check on f2.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.569
So yeah, I think I'm gonna use this as a thumbnail as it's really nice.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:12:00.530
But yeah a very nice victory by Pal Benko defeating Fischer, defeating Tal. He also beat Paul Keres in this tournament.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:08.389
So it was a very nice tournament for Benko. And like I said at one point we will cover the 1962 Curaçao Candidates Tournament.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:15.109
Either as a part of the Tigran Petrosian Saga or as a standalone tournament, we're gonna we're gonna see how it goes.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:21.049
But yeah, that's the game. I do hope you enjoyed it and I would like to apologize for not mentioning

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:25.910
it was a mate in 3 in the previous video. That's the price you pay for not showing all that many puzzles.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:31.120
It just completely slipped my mind, but you know...

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:37.100
Looking on the bright side of things, you know, it's very unlikely that I will make such a mistake again.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.480
So that's that's a plus I guess

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.040
But yeah, like I said, that's the game I do hope you enjoyed it.

